    </div>
    <h1>Fireworks: what the consultation is, and what I said</h1>
    <p class="source-line">Shared by link only. Not listed anywhere on the site.</p>
  </header>

  <article>
    <div class="fw-deadline">
      <strong>Closing date:</strong> 11:59pm on Wednesday 7 October 2026. Anyone can respond, and it takes a few minutes.
    </div>

    <h3>The links</h3>
    <a class="fw-link" href="https://www.rspca.org.uk/getinvolved/campaign/fireworks" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>RSPCA campaign page</span>Send a ready-made response from the RSPCA</a>
    <a class="fw-link alt" href="https://www.gov.uk/government/consultations/fireworks-and-pyrotechnics-in-the-uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Government consultation page</span>Fireworks and pyrotechnics in the UK on GOV.UK</a>
    <a class="fw-link alt" href="https://ditresearch.eu.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_eg0jcixPsRnv1NY" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Respond directly</span>The official online response form</a>

    <h3>What the consultation is</h3>
    <p>The Office for Product Safety and Standards, part of the Department for Business and Trade, opened a public consultation on 16 July 2026. It follows complaints, parliamentary debates and petitions about the effect of fireworks on neighbourhoods, vulnerable people and animals.</p>
    <p>It asks for views on three proposals:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Restricting noisier fireworks so that louder products are only available to professional users.</li>
      <li>Adding products judged to carry unacceptable risks to the existing list of banned pyrotechnics.</li>
      <li>Reviewing the rules for products currently classed as lower risk, so they better reflect the risk they actually pose.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to respond</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Easiest: use the RSPCA page above, which puts a response together for you and sends it to your MP and the consultation team.</li>
      <li>Or use the official online form linked above.</li>
      <li>Or email <strong>fireworksconsultation@businessandtrade.gov.uk</strong> in your own words and say it is a consultation response.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What I asked for</h3>
    <p>I sent my response through the RSPCA route. The three things I asked for were fireworks for the general public capped at 90 decibels, clear and responsible labelling, and a restriction on private firework displays. On the decibel question I said yes, specifically to 90 dB.</p>

    <h3>Why, in short</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The RSPCA found 62% of animal owners say their pet has been negatively affected by fireworks. For dogs the figure is 60%.</li>
      <li>Up to 64% of cat owners report clear signs of stress. In one study 76% of cats hid, 65% fled and 43% froze. Cats are probably more affected than reported, because their reactions are quieter than a dog's.</li>
      <li>Fireworks are unpredictable and intermittent, so animals do not get used to them. Some dogs take days, weeks or months to recover.</li>
      <li>Horses, livestock and wildlife cannot simply be brought indoors with the curtains shut. Horses in particular bolt, and injuries range from cuts to broken limbs.</li>
      <li>Cattle start to react at around 85 dB and show a strong startle response above 90 dB. Pigs should not be exposed to noise above 85 dB under farming welfare regulations. Fireworks can exceed 100 dB.</li>
      <li>Research on birds shows large numbers taking flight at night when they would normally be resting, which costs them energy and causes disorientation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The honest limit</h3>
    <p>A 90 dB cap would help, but it would not solve everything. The unpredictability and the flashing lights would still frighten animals. I said that in my response, because a lower volume is an improvement and not a cure.</p>
